> "Dear Glaucus, come hither and I will put this word in your mind;
> First, one must give dinner to the dogs at the courtyard gates;
> For it is better, for that is what first hears
> The man approaching and the beast entering the enclosure."

Hearing this, Glaucus was pleased with the advice and held him in wonder. After they had dined, they feasted on conversation. As Homer recounted his own wandering and the cities he had visited, Glaucus was amazed to hear it. Then, when it was time for sleep, he rested. The next day, Glaucus intended to go to his master to signify the matters concerning Homer. Having entrusted the guarding of the goats to his fellow slave, he left Homer inside, saying to him that he would return quickly. Having gone down to Bolissus (this village is nearby) and having met with his master, he told the whole truth about Homer, expressing wonder at his arrival and asking what he should do about him. The master gave little weight to the words, but blamed Glaucus as being foolish for receiving and feeding the disabled. Nevertheless, he ordered him to bring the stranger to him. Having come to Homer, Glaucus told him these things and bade him go, for this would be the best course. Homer wished to go; therefore, Glaucus took him and led him to the master. By conversing with Homer,
